    "narrative": "Our optimal lineup for August 21st hinges on two high-floor anchors: **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (76.64) and **Dylan Beavers** (69.52). **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains our premier play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Gordon and a slow delivery that projects well for his opportunistic stolen base potential. Meanwhile, **Dylan Beavers** offers a significant ceiling boost today due to a favorable park environment and a +7% platoon edge against Peralta, effectively making him a cornerstone of our outfield production. We also see high-leverage value in **Lawrence Butler** (69.21) and **JJ Bleday** (68.50), both of whom take advantage of favorable platoon splits\u2014against Wesneski and Rodriguez, respectively\u2014to maintain their status as strong, recommended starts over our depth options.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Justin Crawford** (55.60), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While **Justin Crawford** is a priority add for his speed-focused profile, his projected floor was suppressed today by a bottom-of-the-order assignment (Order #9), which creates an unfavorable ratio of potential plate appearances compared to the rest of the unit. We opted to prioritize the high-volume spots held by **Gleyber Torres** and **Brendan Donovan** at the top of their respective orders. We also remain mindful of the risks posed to **Brendan Donovan** and **Cal Raleigh**; while they are active, both face Boyd, and **Brendan Donovan** in particular faces a steep -15% drag on his score due to the platoon disadvantage, making him the most volatile asset in today's projected lineup. We will monitor the status of **Masyn Winn**, **Jonathan Aranda**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, **Gavin Sheets**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Braden Montgomery** throughout the afternoon to ensure no late-breaking lineup shifts necessitate a pivot.",
